+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for board delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must deliver committed stall-status edits to up to 800 simultaneous browsers with a freshness target of 15 seconds under normal connectivity and opening-time bursts of ~20 edits per second. Editors submit commands over HTTPS to a single authoritative board per event. Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together.
+
+The choice of delivery mechanism determines the shape of the change store, the delivery component, and browser sync logic. It also sets operational cost, vendor coupling, and the amount of connection management the team owns.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+- Meet 15-second freshness target under normal connectivity and burst load. Failing eliminates the option.
+- Support recovery of the latest board after a browser disconnect of up to 20 minutes. Failing eliminates the option.
+- Preserve one authoritative event revision per edit regardless of delivery route. This is an inherited gate from the tenet *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route*.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+- **major** — Low latency and low read amplification. Fewer repeated reads and less lag improves editor experience and reduces load.
+- **major** — Operational independence from realtime vendors. Avoid recurring fees and provider coupling.
+- **moderate** — Connection and reconnect operational burden. Who owns capacity planning, session lifecycle, and catch-up logic.
+- **moderate** — Simplicity of change store. Replayable revisions and reliable publication vs cursor queries.
+- **minor** — Browser implementation complexity. Subscription model vs polling loop.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Description: Browsers poll the server every five seconds for changes. The store exposes cursor queries for incremental reads. Delivery is request/response with no persistent connections.
+
+Analysis:
+- Gates: passes freshness and recovery; authoritative revision preserved.
+- Low latency and low read amplification: weak fit. Polling adds up to five seconds of lag and repeated reads even when idle.
+- Operational independence: strong fit. No vendor dependency, stateless delivery.
+- Connection and reconnect operational burden: strong fit. No session state to manage; browsers reconnect implicitly per request.
+- Simplicity of change store: strong fit. Cursor queries are sufficient; no publication outbox required.
+- Browser implementation complexity: strong fit. Simple polling loop, no reconnect/catch-up state.
+
+Summary: Simplest stateless operation, more repeated reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Description: Server maintains WebSocket sessions to browsers. Edits are published to connected sessions and persisted as replayable revisions. Delivery manages session capacity, reconnects, and reliable publication. Browsers implement reconnect and catch-up logic.
+
+Analysis:
+- Gates: passes freshness and recovery; authoritative revision preserved.
+- Low latency and low read amplification: strong fit. Push updates avoid polling lag and idle reads.
+- Operational independence: strong fit. No realtime vendor; team owns connection capacity.
+- Connection and reconnect operational burden: weak fit. Team owns session lifecycle, capacity planning, and reconnect handling.
+- Simplicity of change store: partial fit. Store must provide replayable revisions and a reliable publication outbox for catch-up.
+- Browser implementation complexity: partial fit. Browsers need reconnect and catch-up logic.
+
+Summary: Fast updates without vendor bill, with ownership of connection capacity and reconnect problems.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+Description: Delivery is wrapped around a managed realtime provider. Publication reconciliation is handled by the provider. Browsers use the provider's subscription model.
+
+Analysis:
+- Gates: passes freshness and recovery; authoritative revision preserved.
+- Low latency and low read amplification: strong fit. Provider optimizes push delivery.
+- Operational independence: weak fit. Recurring fees and provider coupling.
+- Connection and reconnect operational burden: strong fit. Provider manages sessions and reconnects.
+- Simplicity of change store: partial fit. Store must reconcile publication with provider; credentials and outbox management required.
+- Browser implementation complexity: strong fit. Subscription model provided by vendor SDK.
+
+Summary: Quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection ops, but recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets is selected.
+
+It wins on the major criteria of low latency/read amplification and operational independence. The weak fit on connection/reconnect operational burden is accepted explicitly to avoid vendor coupling and recurring fees, aligning with the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* and the preference for avoiding vendor lock-in for a core delivery path.
+
+Five-second HTTPS polling is eliminated by the major latency/read-amplification criterion; the lag and repeated reads are undesirable at opening-time bursts.
+
+Hosted realtime service is eliminated by the major operational independence criterion; recurring fees and provider coupling are not accepted for the pilot.
+
+Implications:
+- Change store must support replayable revisions and reliable publication for catch-up after disconnects up to 20 minutes.
+- Delivery owns WebSocket session management, connection capacity planning, and reconnect handling.
+- Browser sync implements reconnect and catch-up logic for WebSocket delivery.
+- The choice forecloses a stateless polling-only delivery and a vendor-managed realtime path; reversing it would require redesigning store publication, delivery sessions, and browser sync.
